// B6 (handoff-midlang-b6) — THE WORKED EXAMPLE's .mid: THE WORD ABI DERIVED
// FROM THE SIGNATURE, and a STRUCT PARAMETER BY VALUE, imported by name from
// midb6.elm (the reader resolves `import Wsum` to this file, relative to the
// importing file; it is NOT passed on the CLI).
//
// What the program does, in the order plan Q7 names it:
//   * `defun Wsum.add(a : word, b : int, c : bool, g : Big) -> word` — every
//     parameter and the result are WORD-CLASS, so this defun takes the WORD
//     ABI by DERIVATION, not by being listed anywhere: it emits
//     `function l $q_Wsum_x2eadd(l %a0, l %a1, l %a2, l %a3)` with no frame,
//     no :val blit, and a word tail.  `word`/`int`/`bool`/`*T` and a struct
//     VALUE are the word classes; ONE `val` anywhere in the signature would
//     have kept the value ABI.
//   * `word z = a;` — THE RAW-Var CLOSE (the gap B3 pinned): a word-ABI
//     parameter IS a raw register, so rebinding it to another `word` binder is
//     a plain alias of that register (no slot, no box).  Before B6 a
//     parameter had no register at all and this line died at emit with
//     "binder id N is not a raw word".
//   * `g.p.x` / `payload(g.tag, 0)` — a STRUCT PARAMETER BY VALUE, read
//     through the ordinary struct-value rules.  The CALLER passes the struct's
//     address as one `l` register; the CALLEE copies the declared width into
//     its own `alloc8` block at entry (`Big` is 7 words: a `val` field is 5,
//     the nested `Pt` is 2), and the parameter binder names that block.  So
//     `g.p.x = g.p.x + 1;` writes the callee's OWN copy.
//   * `[word](c) * 1000` — the bool PARAMETER read as the 0/1 word it is (a
//     bool argument arrives in an `l` register like every word-class one; the
//     explicit cast is the J12 spelling, and the 1000 makes a lost or
//     mis-registered bool impossible to miss in the printed number).  A
//     two-armed `if` is NOT used here on purpose: a word-ABI defun's tail has
//     no join (`raw-abi defun tail If joins two word-returning branches` is a
//     loud reject), which is its own pre-existing rule and not this unit's.
//
// The printed number packs the four facts a bug in any of the above moves:
//   add's result with the callee's copy mutated(1125) | a SECOND call's result
//   (1125 again — the entry copy is FRESH, so no state survives a call) | the
//   caller's g.p.x (10 — the callee's write did NOT escape)
//   ->  1126125010.
//   * 1125 = 5 (a) + 2 (b) + 11 (g.p.x after the callee's own +1) + 100
//     (g.p.y) + 7 (payload(g.tag, 0)) + 1000 ([word](c)).  A lost/renumbered
//     field, a broken copy width or an unread bool each move it;
//   * the SECOND 1125 is the by-value proof: if the parameter were the
//     caller's storage the second call would read 11 and print 1126;
//   * the trailing 10 is the caller's struct read back AFTER the call — an
//     aliasing (by-reference) parameter would leave 11.
module Wsum
export Wsum.run : val -> val
struct Pt { x : word; y : word; }
struct Big { tag : val; p : Pt; }

defun Wsum.add(a : word, b : int, c : bool, g : Big) -> word {
  word z = a;
  g.p.x = g.p.x + 1;
  return z + b + g.p.x + g.p.y + payload(g.tag, 0) + ([word](c) * 1000);
}

defun Wsum.run(s : val) -> val {
  word k = payload(s, 0);
  Big q;
  q.tag = mknum(7);
  q.p.x = 10;
  q.p.y = 100;
  bool t = [bool](k);
  word r1 = call Wsum.add(k, 2, t, q);
  word r2 = call Wsum.add(k, 2, t, q);
  return mknum((r2 * 1000000) + (r1 * 1000) + q.p.x);
}
